# Expectation record for 10_declarations_and_types/006_enum_definitions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_enum_definitions
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Enumerator value assignment, gaps and negative enumerators
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every enumerator value is kept inside the range of int, because an enumeration's underlying
type is otherwise implementation-defined, so no printed value can depend on that choice.
All arithmetic over enumerator values stays inside the range of int, and the size relation
is printed as a 0/1 comparison rather than as a width.  Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
color=0 1 2
sparse=10 11 20 21
signed=-5 0 7 8
bounded_low_high=-2147483648 2147483647
arith=13 11
classify_folded=300 classify_runtime=300
enum_size_eq_int=1
END
